# Separate Recording Data Plane — CRITICAL

## Separation
Raw call recordings are not part of the application web server filesystem and are not served directly from the Django API.

The application stores metadata and an opaque recording object/reference only:
- call_id;
- tenant_id;
- customer_id;
- artifact_id;
- provider recording reference;
- storage object reference;
- content type/size;
- checksum;
- retention policy/version;
- created/available/deletion timestamps;
- status.

## Recording server boundary
Treat the recording server as a separate security domain.
- Private network/service-to-service access only where possible.
- Strong machine/service authentication.
- TLS for transport.
- Least-privilege service account.
- No database credentials from the application may be reused for recording storage.
- Do not let the recording server query tenant databases directly.

## Access pattern
Tenant users never receive permanent recording URLs.
Preferred flow:
1. authenticate user;
2. authorize tenant + customer + call + artifact;
3. create short-lived, single-purpose access token or signed URL;
4. recording server validates the token/signature;
5. stream/download directly from recording server;
6. audit the access.

Never authorize recording access using only an artifact ID or filename.

## Object namespace
Use non-guessable object identifiers and deterministic tenant partitioning, e.g.:
`tenant/{tenant_id}/calls/{call_id}/artifacts/{artifact_id}`

Do not expose customer names, phone numbers or PII in object names.

## Integrity
- Store checksum/hash for uploaded recording objects.
- Verify object existence and checksum asynchronously.
- Detect orphan metadata and orphan files.
- Do not overwrite immutable historical recordings; new versions require a new artifact/object identity.

## Ingestion
Call completion must not depend on a synchronous upload through the portal/API.
Use an asynchronous artifact workflow:
recording available -> fetch/receive -> integrity verify -> persist metadata -> ready -> emit event.

Failed ingestion must be retryable and reconciled.

## Retention/deletion
Retention is policy-driven and jurisdiction-aware.
- Calculate deletion eligibility from policy metadata.
- Apply legal holds/retention exceptions before deletion.
- Deletion must be auditable.
- Remove inaccessible metadata references only according to the approved retention workflow.
- Never implement "delete call" as a simple row delete.

## Privacy
Never place raw audio, transcript contents or full PII into ordinary application logs, traces or error trackers.
Sensitive artifact access must be auditable.
